From: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: h700: leave VDD-DRAM to the SPL

The H700 family base fixed vdd-dram (AXP717 DCDC3) at 1.1 V with
min = max, so the regulator core moved it to 1.1 V at boot on every
board, whatever DRAM the SPL had found and powered with boot0's voltage
(DDR3 1.5 V, LPDDR3 1.2 V, LPDDR4 1.1 V). The RG CubeXX has LPDDR3
(JEDEC VDD2/VDDQ 1.14-1.30 V): under the heaviest GPU and memory load it
flipped single bits in kernel pointers and page tables, about one God of
War PSP launch in 15-50 crashed or hung the console; at 1.14 V 100
launches ran clean. U-Boot 0008 fixes the same in U-Boot's tree.

A range covering every type keeps the SPL's value (the core only clamps
a voltage outside the range). The stock firmware leaves this rail to
boot0.

Signed-off-by: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024.dts
@@ -413,8 +413,14 @@
 
 			reg_dcdc3: dcdc3 {
 				regulator-always-on;
+				/*
+				 * The SPL sets VDD-DRAM for the DRAM type it
+				 * detected (DDR3 1.5 V, LPDDR3 1.2 V, LPDDR4
+				 * 1.1 V): a range, so Linux keeps that value
+				 * instead of forcing one voltage on every board.
+				 */
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <1100000>;
-				regulator-max-microvolt = <1100000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <1500000>;
 				regulator-name = "vdd-dram";
 			};
 
